          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Consider a prime $p$ of the form $4 t+3$. Show that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ iff $-a$ has order $(p-1) / 2$.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"number_theory\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Let p=4t+3 and put m=(p-1)/2=2t+1, which is odd. All congruences are modulo p.\\n\\nSuppose first that a is a primitive root modulo p. Then ord_p(a)=p-1=2m. Since (a^m)^2=a^{2m}\\\\equiv1 and a^m\\\\not\\\\equiv1, primality and oddness of p imply a^m\\\\equiv-1. Therefore\\n(-a)^m=(-1)^m a^m\\\\equiv(-1)(-1)=1.\\nIf (-a)^n\\\\equiv1, then a^{2n}=(-a)^{2n}\\\\equiv1. Hence 2m\\\\mid2n, so m\\\\mid n. Thus ord_p(-a)=m=(p-1)/2.\\n\\nConversely, suppose ord_p(-a)=m. Then\\n a^m=(-1)^m(-a)^m\\\\equiv-1,\\nso a^{2m}\\\\equiv1. If a^n\\\\equiv1, then (-a)^{2n}=a^{2n}\\\\equiv1, and therefore m\\\\mid2n. Since m is odd, m\\\\mid n; write n=qm. Now\\n1\\\\equiv a^n=(a^m)^q\\\\equiv(-1)^q,\\nso q is even. Hence 2m\\\\mid n. Consequently ord_p(a)=2m=p-1, and a is a primitive root modulo p.\"}",
